00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.070
From Disney bounders who go to the park

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.470
in outfits inspired by
their favorite characters

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.990
to rich TikTokers moving
into a gated community

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.190
of Disney world mansions,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.510
the folks at karaoke night

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.710
who bring the mood down

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.650
with a passionate rendition
of "God Helped the Outcast

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.067
from the Hunchback of Notre Dame."

00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:49.900
♪ God ♪

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.380
Enthusiasm for Disney

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.230
just seems to rub the
general public the wrong way,

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.287
and to be fair and balanced,

00:05:03.287 --> 00:05:05.497
I confess to once singing this song

00:05:05.497 --> 00:05:07.500
"I'll Make a Man Out of You

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.970
from the movie Mulan"

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.940
at a karaoke bar in Northfield, Minnesota.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.040
I think it was the summer of 2009,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.930
and to all who were in attendance,

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.340
I formally apologize.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.430
So the backlash over this
food less Disney wedding

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.530
eventually spurred professor
of religious studies

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:26.370
Dr. Jodi Eichler-Levine to
bravely step into the discourse.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.860
She urged the very online folks

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.310
to have a little compassion
for Disney adults. Why?

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.320
Because according to Eichler-Levine,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.810
Disney adulting is akin
to practicing a religion,

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:39.150
and we ought to be a little more sensitive

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.340
to people's sacred experiences

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.930
at the church of Mickey Mouse.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:47.160
In the name of the Minnie,
Mickey, and Donald Duck. Amen.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:48.510
Don't put that in the video.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.340
In her sympathetic Twitter
thread, she writes,

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.830
if we measure religion
not by its truth-claims

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.690
but by its power in people's lives,

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.180
then Disney is as much
a religion as anything.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.470
It is, at the very least,

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.870
a sight of meaning and human fellowship,

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.160
even if you hate it.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.260
She argues that people embrace Disney

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.820
for various personal reasons.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.980
Maybe Disney World was the only place

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.480
where your parents ever seemed happy.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.760
Maybe you watched frozen
on your first date

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:12.593
with your spouse,

00:06:12.593 --> 00:06:14.130
or maybe you found solace

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.220
dumping your favorite uncle's
ashes on Splash Mountain.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.420
Please don't try the last one.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.530
It's very much illegal.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.930
But this argument caused many to wonder.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.360
Was Eichler Levine defending Disney adults

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.270
or offering an unflattering diagnosis

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.620
of their spiritual condition?

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.230
And does Disney fandom really
have that much in common

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:32.430
with religious worship?

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.230
Let's take a step back

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:37.800
and start by approaching
Disney adults as a fandom.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.980
Scholar Cornel Sandvoss
writes that fandom is defined

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.680
by the regular emotionally
involved consumption

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.050
of a given popular narrative or text.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.730
So Disney lovers who have cried

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.370
over Mufasa's death 3000 times

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:51.990
certainly would seem to fit the bill.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.450
Now, the link between fandom and religion

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.060
has long been discussed
in academic circles.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.750
In fact, the word fan even
has religious etymology

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.150
coming from the Latin word fanaticus,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.250
which meant attendant of the temple

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.683
or the fanus or fanus,

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:12.960
fan of, I'll say it, fan of the
anus is what it sounds like,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.220
and that's fine.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.053
And, of course,

00:07:15.053 --> 00:07:17.880
deeply religious people
are often called fanatics.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:18.713
It's also the name

00:07:18.713 --> 00:07:22.230
of the Philadelphia Phillie's
very chaotic looking mascot.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.300
As scholar Vernon Chadwick argues,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.700
the Latin root of religion, religio,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:30.090
is defined not in terms
of the truth of dogma

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.330
but the strength of communal
bonds forms by shared belief.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:34.830
Such a community can manifest

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:38.310
around shared rights of worship
or sacred objects or sites.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.410
Chadwick explains that taking care of

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.330
and maintaining these
elements of a religion

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.580
easily become ends in themselves,

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.770
independent of explicitly defined

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.420
and defended articles of belief.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:50.760
This is all to say
religion could be viewed

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.000
less as what you believe
and more as what you do,

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.340
and this makes sense as
many organized religions

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:57.810
place a big emphasis on religion

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.750
being about acts of
service and lived virtues.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.460
Friend of the channel
soaring character guard

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.700
also help this view calling
Christianity a practice

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.620
rather than just being a system

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.510
of like bullet pointed beliefs.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.160
And folks have long been squeaked out

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:14.460
by what intense fans
do in their communities

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.650
specifically because of
the seeming proximity

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:17.910
to religious experience.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.220
For example, when Elvis's gyrating hips

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.380
made children fall into tearful hysterics,

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.180
some parents got real nervous

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.220
because huge emotional reactions

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.830
were ideally meant for church revivals,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.650
not sexual awakenings
via the Ed Sullivan Show.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.000
Christian groups even worried

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.710
that Christian contemporary music

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:37.050
would distract its fans
from worshiping God

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:39.000
and lead them towards secularism.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.670
Instead it just made millions
and millions of dollars

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.610
and produce gems like this.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:46.410
♪ If you do drugs and
you think you're cool, ♪

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.930
you need to come to sunday school.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.480
What's more, the
conventional language we use

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:54.570
to talk about fandom
certainly invokes religion.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:56.580
We call niche fandoms, cults,

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.130
and talk about our culture
of celebrity worship.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.110
So it's no wonder scholars of religion,

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:02.700
media, and fandom alike

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.860
have grappled for decades
with the question,

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.200
do fandoms constitute a religion?

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.960
A scholar Mark Duffett put
it because fan phenomena

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.840
can sometimes look similar to
moments of religious ecstasy,

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.550
the idea that fandom is like a religion

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.260
is a ready-made metaphor.

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.060
I also like the notion
of religious ecstasy,

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:19.680
like going to church on ecstasy,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.850
that sounds fun.

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.410
Don't do it, but do it.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.360
Duffett notes that some
academic researchers

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.880
who subscribe to this
interpretation of fandom

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.800
treated fans with empathy

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.600
while others use the analogy

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:35.460
to stereotype and lambaste
fans as servile and misguided;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.640
that is to say to treat
fandom as a religious practice

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.790
sometimes means to pathologize the fan.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:43.980
IE to regard or treat them

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.590
as psychologically abnormal or unhealthy.

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:48.450
It's worth noting that
some forms of fandom

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.760
are basically treated as
normal by our society,

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.920
while others are viewed with skepticism.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.570
Nobody's gonna call you a cultist

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.910
for wearing your Tom
Brady Jersey for a week

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:58.140
leading up to the super bowl,

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.510
but if you carry around your
replica of Thor's hammer

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.490
for a week leading up
to the new MCU movie,

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:04.980
people will probably have thoughts.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.230
And we certainly see
plenty of pathologizing

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.940
when it comes to Disney adults,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.620
especially on the internet.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:12.180
Articles that titles like,

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.820
Disney adults are the most
terrifying people on the planet,

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:17.340
and they need to be
stopped, regularly pop up.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.260
With assertions that Disney adults pension

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:20.850
for getting off on nostalgia

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.520
is worse than being an
actual serial killer.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:24.660
Though, come to think of it,

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:27.768
a serial killer who is also a Disney adult

00:10:27.768 --> 00:10:31.350
would be the most nightmarish
thing I could ever imagine.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:32.880
But snarky Twitter users

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.490
didn't invent this kind
of cultural pathologizing.

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:38.430
Many early scholars of then
burgeoning mass culture

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.190
approached early fans much the same way,

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:44.820
specifically a Marxist powerhouse
of German intellectuals

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.740
who formed what's called
the Frankfurt school,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:49.710
sort of like the goof troupe
of cranky philosophers.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:52.020
Members included dudes
like Theodor Adorno,

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:53.790
Walter Benjamin, and Max Wertheimer,

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.650
who collectively pioneered the discipline

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.780
we now know as critical theory.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:10:59.340
Critical theory views philosophy

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.410
and social theory's primary purposes

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.630
as being tools to critique social

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.430
and political structures of power,

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:06.750
and when several of these folks

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.910
fled Nazi Germany for America,

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.640
they found plenty to
critique about its culture.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:12.540
Writing for Vox,

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:13.830
Sean Illing explains,

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:16.050
they saw the yolk of capitalist ideology

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.570
wherever they looked in films, in radio,

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:20.460
in popular music, in literature,

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.860
Adorno called this the culture industry,

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:23.880
and Illing notes,

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.070
he cautioned that it
blurred the distinction

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:27.360
between truth and fiction

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.940
between the commercial and the political.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.460
As journalists and authors
Stuart Jeffries explains,

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:35.250
these scholars wondered
why a socialist revolution

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:36.660
had swept across Russia

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:40.170
while failing to manifest in
advanced industrialized nations

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.360
like Germany and the United States.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.580
He writes, "they concluded that culture

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.010
and the use of the media
was the primary tool

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:48.690
for oppressing the masses

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:52.020
without the masses realizing
that they're being oppressed."

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:55.680
And so the argument went mass
culture with its flashy lights

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.010
and captivating narratives
was indoctrinating people

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.080
into dominant ideologies.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:02.160
IE ways of thinking

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.320
that wouldn't threaten or upset

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.230
those in physicians of
economic and political power.

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:09.240
The culture industry thus caused people

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:12.300
to unconsciously and
unquestioningly accept

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:14.570
what the people in power
wanted them to believe.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:16.260
In this way, these scholars thought

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.050
that culture was essentially
taking the place of

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.960
and functioning like
an organized religion.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.580
Note that during this time,

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.980
religion was undergoing a decline in power

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.300
over the national psyche.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:28.717
As Adorno and Wertheimer wrote,

00:12:28.717 --> 00:12:30.210
"the sociological theory

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:31.680
that the loss of the support

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.540
of objectively established religion

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.190
has led to cultural chaos

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:36.540
is disproved every day;

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:40.410
for culture now impresses the
same stamp on everything."

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:42.660
That is to say culture
was indoctrinating people

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:44.880
into the sorts of dominant ideologies,

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.150
which were once exclusively
the terrain of religion.

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.580
This development upset
these guys to no end,

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:52.890
and Adorno even pen a hateful paper

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:57.453
about the insidious national
disease of jazz music,

00:12:58.500 --> 00:12:59.343
like who doesn't like jazz music.

00:12:59.343 --> 00:13:01.350
It's it's really nice.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:02.370
What's interesting though is,

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:04.218
is who made jazz music at that time?

00:13:04.218 --> 00:13:06.720
(trumpet blaring)

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:07.830
So God only knows

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:10.200
what he would think of
contemporary Disney culture

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:12.330
with all its mouse ears, season passes,

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:14.610
and Disney-themed funeral companies.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.740
Oh, and we're not making that last one up,

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:20.430
if I ever die and you
(beep) dare to bury me

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.011
in Mickey Mouse ears,

00:13:22.011 --> 00:13:25.440
I'm gonna come back as a
hybrid zombie and ghost,

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.210
and just haunt the (beep) outta you.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:28.950
Anyway, going forward,

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:32.030
scholars wouldn't all share
Adorno's scorn for mass culture.

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:33.150
In the sixties,

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:35.550
England's Birmingham
School of Cultural Studies

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:36.930
started to take fans,

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:39.810
especially working class
ones, more seriously.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:41.400
And in the eighties and nineties,

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.710
fandom scholarship really took a turn away

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:45.930
from those old unflattering portraits.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.060
That's thanks largely
to the work of academics

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.150
like Henry Jenkins, who were also fans.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:53.880
These acafans were already immersed

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:56.130
in the fandom cultures they were studying

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:57.720
and were thus more sympathetic.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.540
Instead of diagnosing fans as docile

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:02.760
and semi brainwashed from afar,

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:06.840
these scholars asked fans
how and why they interacted

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:08.250
with their chosen media texts.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:10.020
Also, I would like to announce

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.180
that I'm officially now an acafan

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.101
of the television shows, "Scrubs."

00:14:14.101 --> 00:14:17.654
- Okay, Sasha, let's see
what your new engine can do.

00:14:17.654 --> 00:14:20.460
(motor engine roaring)

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:21.690
- They asserted that fans

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:23.790
weren't uncritically absorbing ideologies

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:27.900
in mass media and blindly
adhering to the status quo.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.400
Rather, they saw the way fans

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.220
would poach ideas and
characters and stories

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:33.360
from the things they liked.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.490
They would then remake and remix them

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:37.230
to align with their own values,

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:40.590
often to tell us subversive
countercultural stories.

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:43.020
If you lived through Tumblr in the 2010,

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:45.840
you've seen enough Dustin
O and Kirken Splunk Splash

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:47.790
to know exactly what they mean,

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:51.030
even if I have no (beep)
idea what I just said.

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:52.653
- I love you.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.010
- This poaching can also be seen

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:58.290
in some activities of Disney adults.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.690
Take Garrett Smith and Jason Bitner,

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:02.310
who dressed as Woody and Buzz

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.860
for their elaborate toy
story themed wedding

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:07.500
remaking Disney cannon
to fit their love story.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:09.330
I can only hope it was officiated

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:11.640
by a friend in a Mr. Potato head outfit.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.400
This wave of scholars took
a utopian view of fandom,

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.920
and it felt it was
important not to see fans

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.380
as cultish or worshipful

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.800
but as enthusiastic
thoughtful consumers of media.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:24.330
- A lot of us appreciate the fact

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.730
that it presents a very
positive view of the future.

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:28.860
It doesn't show the earth
being devastated by wars.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:30.510
It shows us having finally figured out

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:32.430
how to solve our worst problems.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:34.500
- That's because these scholars and fans

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.950
saw religiosity as
equivalent to irrationality

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.260
and painted religion
as an unfounded belief

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.980
in something supernatural
and ultimately not real.

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:45.720
And Henry Jenkins's phrasing

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:47.700
calling fans religious paints them

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:49.470
as prone to false beliefs,

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:52.830
orgiastic excess, possession, and madness.

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.090
And clearly, he's talking

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:57.180
about Disney character
cosplay orgys there,

00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:58.620
and I would like to see it.

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.260
Labeling fans as religious
fanatics, he said,

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.170
can also be inherently
othering and stigmatizing.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:06.480
It makes them out to be obsessive devotees

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.390
who look at Captain
Kirk as a literal deity

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:11.610
and pray to Obi-Wan Kenobi for wisdom.

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:14.850
These scholars tried to distance
fan culture from religion

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:15.960
and define fans

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.260
as rational autonomous
media consumers and critics.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:20.227
As Jenkins puts it,

00:16:20.227 --> 00:16:21.960
"as a religion you bring back

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:23.760
in this notion of literal belief,

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.340
and it implies that fans are
unable to separate fiction

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:29.520
from reality or that they
supposedly act on the text

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:31.410
as if it were literally true."

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:34.380
However, this, according
to many religious scholars,

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:36.480
is a problematic view of religion.

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:39.660
For one, it paints
religious people as childish

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:42.090
or rational and disconnected
from the real world.

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:44.460
It asserts that they
don't question their text

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:46.590
and take them as absolute truth.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:49.410
What's more, it maps Western
conceptions of religion,

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:50.850
specifically Christianity,

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.799
onto hugely diverse cultural traditions

00:16:52.799 --> 00:16:55.860
that may bear little to
no resemblance to it.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:58.280
Not all religious movements
require literal beliefs

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:00.240
in the supernatural or deities.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:02.490
Not all religions make grand truth-claims

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:04.650
or passing a metaphysical realm,

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:07.920
or even have a cohesive set of traditions.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.600
Scholars Carole M. Cusack

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:11.797
and Venetia Laura Delano
Robertson explains,

00:17:11.797 --> 00:17:13.080
"religion is today studied

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:15.270
as not exclusively a tradition-based

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:16.740
or faith-based practice,

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:18.900
but a rationale, an experience.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:20.400
So when scholars of religion

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.800
compare the experiences of Disney adults

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:24.630
to those of religious adherence,

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:27.660
they're not asserting that a
Disney filed drinking water

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.120
from each of the parks
fountains believes themself

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:33.090
to be consuming the literal
trans substantiated blood

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:35.220
of his lord and savior Donald Duck

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.380
nor do they mean that
someone brought to tears

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:38.820
by a fireworks display

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:41.910
truly believes themselves to
be witnessing fairy magic.

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:43.680
Instead they see Disney lovers

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.590
as participating in a secularized version

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:47.550
of religious fervor

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:49.860
in which they encounter a deeply personal

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.420
and experiential connection

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:53.370
to something bigger than themselves.

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.430
So clearly, there's been
some scholarly disagreement.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.847
Some justify viewing
fandoms embrasive culture

00:17:58.847 --> 00:18:01.380
as a kind of replacement for religion,

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:05.040
others think that fans are
doing something very different.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.840
So with scholars disagreeing

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:08.790
on the question of whether or not Disney

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:10.890
can properly be considered a religion,

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:13.170
we think the best way to get
to the bottom of this question

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:15.839
is to step back and
reconsider the question,

00:18:15.839 --> 00:18:18.060
what makes a religion anyway,

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:20.400
and then we can see how
much overlap there is

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:22.950
between organized religion
as it's commonly practiced

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:26.760
and the habits and practices
of the Disney adult community.

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:27.990
One thing that's for sure

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:29.790
is that there's no organized religion

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:32.010
that says it's cool to
leave menacing comments

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:35.100
on the wedding photos of
a gentle YouTube post,

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:37.215
but we'll get to all that in part two,

00:18:37.215 --> 00:18:38.817
where we'll also be asking,

00:18:38.817 --> 00:18:40.680
is there anything that Disney adults

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:43.650
can tell us about contemporary society

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.810
other than that the most
important thing you can do

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:46.920
when planning a wedding

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:50.730
is keep your guest well fed
and with fresh drinks in hand.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.800
So check back later this
week to watch us try

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.710
and answer all those
questions once and for all,

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.810
assuming, of course, that a
gang of irate Disney adults

00:18:57.810 --> 00:18:59.040
don't kidnap me first.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.070
So if you see me blink
twice in the next video,

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:03.780
I'm in trouble, call for help.

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:04.613
In the meantime,

00:19:04.613 --> 00:19:06.660
let us know your thoughts
on the church of Walt

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:07.740
in the comments.

